  'wiki-w_ideology.json': [
    {n:'Liberalism',la:51.51,ln:-0.10,sub:'17th-18th c. · Europe',desc:'Political philosophy emphasizing individual rights, liberty, equality, and consent of the governed. Rooted in Enlightenment thinkers: Locke, Mill, Rousseau. Supports democracy, free markets (classical) or social welfare (modern). Foundation of Western political systems.',fact:'Individual rights · Liberty · Locke, Mill, Rousseau · Democracy · Classical & modern variants',_cat:'w_ideology',_quality:'good'},
    {n:'Socialism',la:49.75,ln:6.64,sub:'19th c. · Europe',desc:'Political and economic theory advocating social ownership of means of production. Developed by Marx, Engels, Bernstein. Variants: democratic socialism, communism, social democracy. Aims to reduce inequality. Influential in labor movements worldwide.',fact:'Social ownership · Marx, Engels · Democratic socialism · Communism · Social democracy',_cat:'w_ideology',_quality:'good'},
    {n:'Conservatism',la:51.51,ln:-0.13,sub:'18th-19th c. · Europe',desc:'Political philosophy emphasizing tradition, order, stability, and established institutions. Reaction to French Revolution. Key thinkers: Burke, de Maistre. Supports gradual change, authority, private property. Variants: fiscal, social, nationalist.',fact:'Tradition & order · Burke · Gradual change · Private property · Authority',_cat:'w_ideology',_quality:'good'},
    {n:'Fascism',la:44.50,ln:11.35,sub:'20th c. · Europe',desc:'Far-right authoritarian ideology emphasizing nationalism, dictatorship, suppression of opposition. Developed by Mussolini. Features: cult of leader, militarism, anti-communism, corporatism. Nazi Germany was extreme variant. Rejected democracy and individual rights.',fact:'Authoritarian nationalism · Mussolini · Cult of leader · Anti-democracy · Nazi variant',_cat:'w_ideology',_quality:'good'},
    {n:'Communism',la:50.27,ln:5.34,sub:'19th-20th c. · Europe',desc:'Revolutionary socialist ideology aiming for classless society. Based on Marx and Engels\' Communist Manifesto (1848). Lenin adapted for Russia (1917). Features: abolition of private property, planned economy, vanguard party. USSR, China, Cuba as examples.',fact:'Marx & Engels · Classless society · Lenin · USSR, China, Cuba · Abolition of private property',_cat:'w_ideology',_quality:'good'},
    {n:'Democracy',la:37.97,ln:23.72,sub:'5th c. BCE · Athens, Greece',desc:'System of government where power rests with citizens through voting. Originated in Ancient Athens. Features: free elections, rule of law, protection of rights, separation of powers. Variants: direct, representative, parliamentary, presidential.',fact:'Athens 5th c. BCE · Rule of law · Free elections · Separation of powers · Representative & direct',_cat:'w_ideology',_quality:'good'},
    {n:'Nationalism',la:48.86,ln:2.30,sub:'18th-19th c. · Europe',desc:'Ideology emphasizing loyalty to one\'s nation. Emerged with French Revolution. Key theorists: Herder, Mazzini, Renan. Self-determination principle. Can be civic (based on citizenship) or ethnic (based on ancestry).',fact:'Nation loyalty · French Revolution · Herder, Mazzini · Self-determination · Civic vs ethnic',_cat:'w_ideology',_quality:'good'},
    {n:'Anarchism',la:46.20,ln:6.14,sub:'19th c. · Europe',desc:'Political philosophy opposing all forms of coercive hierarchy and the state. Key thinkers: Proudhon, Bakunin, Kropotkin. Advocates voluntary cooperation, mutual aid, decentralization.',fact:'Anti-state · Proudhon, Bakunin, Kropotkin · Mutual aid · Decentralization · Voluntary cooperation',_cat:'w_ideology',_quality:'good'},
    {n:'Feminism',la:40.71,ln:-74.01,sub:'19th-20th c. · Global',desc:'Advocacy for women\'s rights and gender equality. Three waves: suffrage (19th/early 20th), reproductive rights/equality (1960s), intersectionality (1990s+). Key figures: Wollstonecraft, Stanton, de Beauvoir, Friedan.',fact:'Gender equality · Suffrage movement · Three waves · Wollstonecraft, de Beauvoir · Intersectionality',_cat:'w_ideology',_quality:'good'},
    {n:'Environmentalism',la:42.36,ln:-71.06,sub:'19th-20th c. · Global',desc:'Movement protecting natural environment from human impact. Roots in conservation (Muir) and preservation (Thoreau). Modern wave sparked by Rachel Carson (Silent Spring, 1962). Climate activism, biodiversity protection.',fact:'Conservation & preservation · Silent Spring (1962) · Climate activism · Sustainability · Biodiversity',_cat:'w_ideology',_quality:'good'},
    {n:'Secularism',la:48.86,ln:2.30,sub:'17th-18th c. · Europe',desc:'Principle of separation of religion from state. Rooted in Enlightenment. Key thinkers: Locke, Voltaire, Jefferson. French laïcité, US separation of church and state. Aims to ensure religious freedom and state neutrality.',fact:'Separation of church & state · Locke, Voltaire, Jefferson · Laïcité · Religious freedom · State neutrality',_cat:'w_ideology',_quality:'good'},
    {n:'Pluralism',la:40.71,ln:-74.01,sub:'20th c. · Global',desc:'Political theory recognizing diversity of groups and interests in society. Multiple centers of power. Unlike totalism which concentrates power. Democratic pluralism encourages competing interests, civil society, and consensus-building.',fact:'Diversity of groups · Multiple power centers · Civil society · Consensus-building · Anti-totalism',_cat:'w_ideology',_quality:'good'},
  ],
